# A chapter carrying a GRAPH — the shape a table or a list cannot hold: nodes joined by
# relations, drawn as a diagram instead of a nesting. The body element is a yamlover node tagged
# with the xyflow schema, and everything under it is ordinary yamlover (docs/language/model/graph).
#
# What the drawing shows, one rule at a time:
#   - a node's scalar SELF-VALUE is written inside its box; a node with no value is a bare circle;
#   - every relation is titled by its ORDINAL and, when it has one, its KEY (`2: review`), so a
#     position-only relation shows a number alone — and is drawn lighter for it;
#   - a `*` dereference is an EDGE, never a copy (docs/language/pointers/dereference): the relation
#     lands straight on the target, dashed;
#   - an `&` anchor pushes a membership the other way (docs/language/pointers/anchors) and is dashed
#     to its own rhythm;
#   - a `*`/`&` end OUTSIDE the drawn subtree becomes a dashed stub named by its path.
#
# The tag also opens a DOCUMENT (the engine's boundary.ts): a `:`-scoped reference inside the graph
# resolves from the graph's own root, so the content reads the same wherever it is pasted. Stepping
# out of it takes an explicit `..`.
!!<*yamlover: $defs: chapter>
Pipeline, drawn
description: One graph, every edge kind.
- The pipeline below is a **graph**, not a nesting — the same document the tree shows as keys.
- !!<*yamlover: $defs: xyflow>
  ingest: raw documents
  validate:
    rules: strict
    quarantine:                 # no value — an empty circle
  classify:
    - fast lane                 # position-only relations: titled `0` and `1`
    - slow lane
  review:
    reads: *: validate          # a `*` dereference — dashed, straight onto the validate node
  exports:
    latest: the shelf
  publish: to the shelf
    &: exports: shipped         # an `&` anchor — publish ALSO lives at exports: shipped
  glossary: *..: description    # `..` steps OUT of the graph — a dashed stub
- Read the numbers as positions and the names as keys; both title the same relation.
